WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:06.130
Dismembered limbs, exploding eyeballs, and
one very uncomfortable torture scene.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.111
How these movies managed to avoid an R rating
is anyone's guess.

00:00:10.111 --> 00:00:14.750
"The Dark Knight" is an amazing film, and
widely considered to be one of the best superhero

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.123
movies of all time.

00:00:16.123 --> 00:00:20.880
Christopher Nolan's gritty installment in
the Batman franchise is brilliantly done,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.660
and the Joker was one of Heath Ledger's best
roles, helping to drive Batman to a darker,

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.000
more complex understanding of himself and
the nature of good and evil.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.480
"And here we go."

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.814
It's a little surprising, then, that the film
didn't score an R rating.

00:00:36.814 --> 00:00:41.840
"The Dark Knight" doesn't shy away from violence,
even if it isn't particularly bloody.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.716
Case in point?

00:00:42.716 --> 00:00:44.530
The infamous pencil trick.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.280
Even Wally Pfister, the film's cinematographer,
is surprised that didn't earn "The Dark Knight"

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.084
an R rating.

00:00:50.084 --> 00:00:51.280
He told Vulture:

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.530
"It's kind of shocking that 'The Dark Knight'
ended up being PG-13.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:57.890
[...] Somehow, [Nolan] always had some magic
with the MPAA.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:04.140
Lo and behold, he has the pencil and I'm like,
'You are not getting the PG-13 with that.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.130
There's no way this is going in.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.669
He's driving this pencil through a guy's head!'

00:01:08.669 --> 00:01:09.928
And I was wrong."

00:01:09.928 --> 00:01:12.579
So how'd they manage to avoid the R rating?

00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:15.119
By using the pencil trick as a joke.

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:16.119
According to Pfister:

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:20.240
"I thought, If it has the right amount of
levity, we’ll sell this and it won’t come

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.250
off as being violent but it’ll come off
as being a magic trick and it’ll come off

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.512
as being a punchline.

00:01:25.512 --> 00:01:26.388
And it was!"

00:01:26.388 --> 00:01:27.388
"Ta-da!"

00:01:27.831 --> 00:01:33.590
"Taken," a 2008 action-thriller starring Liam
Neeson, was a box office star that earned

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:40.140
over $226 million worldwide, launched countless
internet memes, and firmly ingrained itself

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.220
in popular culture.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.780
It might seem as though the film would be
prime R-rated material, but it actually managed

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.420
to pull in a PG-13 rating.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:52.810
"Taken" could have easily been rated R. It
has shocking violence, including an alarming

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.754
torture scene that had to be heavily edited
for the PG-13 version.

00:01:56.754 --> 00:01:57.945
"Wake up!

00:01:57.945 --> 00:01:59.413
I need you to be focused!"

00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:04.310
The extended "harder cut" features even more
graphic violence in this scene: Neeson's Bryan

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.110
Mills stabs a man with two metal nails so
he can electrocute him.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:13.310
"Taken" also deals with human trafficking,
showing moments of hard drug use and implied

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.750
sexual assault with several female characters.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.770
Realistic and extreme portrayals of violence
are not acceptable in the MPAA PG-13 rating,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.340
and gushing blood is what usually pushes it
into R territory.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.860
But even with all the violent hand-to-hand
combat scenes and bullets flying everywhere,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.250
"Taken" managed to pull off the more financially
lucrative PG-13 rating, probably because they

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.557
kept most of the blood and gore to a minimum.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.090
The found footage horror genre has always
been compelling; who doesn't love a freaky

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:45.600
dash of realism in their scary movie?

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.200
"Cloverfield," the first in a multi-film franchise,
premiered in 2008, seemingly just another

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.060
teen monster movie with too many jump cuts.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:00.920
However, "Cloverfield" was a massive box office
hit, bringing in over $172 million worldwide,

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.030
and landing on a few "Best Horror Films of
the Decade" lists.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.941
Yet, this surprisingly frightening film features
more than a few scenes of shocking gore; the

00:03:08.941 --> 00:03:12.739
gruesome nature of the movie probably should
have earned it a higher rating.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:17.650
One scene, in particular, stands out, as Lizzy
Caplan's Marlena begins bleeding from the

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.390
eyes and then, quite literally, explodes.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.420
The film's monster, which isn't shown to the
audience until almost halfway through, is

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.370
the size of a city building, meaning that
its favorite thing to do is eat people.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.920
Without any concern for our delicate viewer
sensibilities, the monster crunches away through

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.160
a few humans.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.500
Usually, this much gore would be enough to
put the brakes on a PG-13 rating; however,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.500
producer J.J.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.290
Abrams somehow managed to keep the teen-friendly
rating.

00:03:44.366 --> 00:03:49.488
Live-action motion picture capture became
incredibly popular during the early 2000s.

00:03:49.488 --> 00:03:54.610
But when a cinematic adaptation of the ancient
English epic poem "Beowulf" was released in

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.930
2007, only three other films had used the
technology.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:03.709
"Beowulf" starred Angelina Jolie, John Malkovich,
and Anthony Hopkins — or, more accurately,

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:04.999
CGI versions of them.

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:10.140
Even though the film is entirely CGI, it's
incredibly realistic… for the most part.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.780
So when critics and viewers left the cinema,
they left wondering, "Was that Angelina Jolie

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.820
in the nude or just her character?"

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:22.520
It's a scene that Jolie later admitted made
her feel exposed, despite its animated classification.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:29.420
In fact, the actor told reporters at the premiere
that the film's MPAA rating surprised her, saying:

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.843
"It's remarkable it has the rating it has.

00:04:31.843 --> 00:04:35.750
It's quite an extraordinary film, and some
of it shocked me."

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:42.960
"Beowulf" is incredibly violent, more so than
its original poem: spears, arms, heads, blood,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.419
and dismembered limbs rushed at the audience,
because it was also shot in 3D.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:52.639
The film was so shocking to audiences that
in the UK, where it was given a "12A" rating,

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:57.139
the British Board of Film Classification received
more complaints about "Beowulf" than any other

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:58.281
movie that year.

00:04:58.357 --> 00:05:02.807
Not only does the film feature plenty of nudity
— and the occasional incest joke — but

00:05:02.807 --> 00:05:08.400
the amount of blood that spurts out of bodies,
along with various floating organs, is overwhelmingly

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.720
gratuitous, animated or not.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.270
Going into a movie about a giant ape on an
island filled with other giant monsters, you

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:21.370
have to expect at least a little bit of monster
mayhem. 2017's "Kong: Skull Island" doesn’t

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.750
disappoint when it comes to creature gore.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.889
Seeing a few soldiers get eaten and thrown
around, or a lizard forcibly losing its tongue

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:29.660
shouldn't come as a surprise.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.540
What is surprising, however, is just how graphic
it all is when it happens.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.590
So much of the film is wanton violence, monster
dismemberment, and the over-the-top display

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.350
of blood arcing through the jungle.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:45.070
And while a giant spider impaling a man isn't
going to keep audiences from the theater,

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:48.228
it should maybe come with a harder rating
than PG-13.

00:05:48.299 --> 00:05:49.299
"Whoa whoa whoa whoa…

00:05:49.299 --> 00:05:52.163
no, no no, no need for that, come on now."

00:05:52.163 --> 00:05:56.470
For director Jordan Vogt-Roberts, however,
the violence in the film makes sense.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:00.900
He's cited "Apocalypse Now" as one of his
main influences, while also packing "Skull

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.569
Island" full of not-so-subtle references to
other Vietnam classics.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:09.690
Regardless, the sight of a man's arm being
ripped off usually remains in "Rated R" territory.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.419
Maybe it's its borderline cartoonishness that
gives "Kong: Skull Island" a pass.

00:06:13.815 --> 00:06:18.599
Some might be surprised to see the blockbuster
Bond film "Casino Royale" on this list, but

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:22.490
there are more than a few scenes that call
its PG-13 rating into question.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:28.190
In 2006, "Casino Royale" felt like a groundbreaking
new take on James Bond: It was grittier and

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.820
more emotional with a Bond struggling with
the violence inherent in his role, a self-awareness

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.569
not seen before in the usually carefree womanizer.

00:06:35.569 --> 00:06:41.319
It was also a box office smash, earning over
$605 million worldwide.

00:06:41.319 --> 00:06:46.210
With the darker themes came more graphic and
explicit scenes of violence, particularly

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.740
in the film's intense portrayal of torture.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:53.530
Towards the end of the film, Bond is captured
by Le Chiffre and tortured in a particularly

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:54.668
memorable scene.

00:06:54.668 --> 00:07:00.169
And, while we won't get into specifics, in
order to get its PG-13 rating, Bond's torture,

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:03.680
along with several other fight scenes, had
to be cut down.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.840
But should you come across the extended edition
of the film, be prepared for Bond's howls

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.350
of pain to carry on for longer, Le Chiffre
to make some unsavory comments about Bond's

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.389
physique, and a little more blood in the fight
scenes.

00:07:15.389 --> 00:07:19.530
Much like Bond's escape from Le Chiffre's
torture, "Casino Royale" barely made it out

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:20.761
without an R rating.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.770
Sam Raimi, a noted horror and noir aficionado,
wrote and directed the 2011 film, "Drag Me

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:27.503
to Hell."

00:07:27.503 --> 00:07:32.330
This campy supernatural horror film was mainly
marketed as a teen flick, filled with young

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:37.300
actors like Alison Lohman and Justin Long,
shock-value special effects, and dark humor.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.509
And for the most part, "Drag Me to Hell" delivers
on that score.

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:45.890
It also delivers on the gore, and many of
the violent scenes in the film are shockingly bloody.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:50.680
The most famous involves Mrs. Ganush, an old
woman hellbent on revenge after being denied

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.650
an extension on her mortgage loan, and Christine,
the young bank teller who denied it.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:00.400
When Mrs. Ganush shoves her arm down Christine's
throat — literally, Christine manages to

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.120
overpower her using an anvil.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:08.570
What happens next is equal parts gross and
comedic: the anvil crushes Mrs. Ganush's head,

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.680
causing her eyes to explode and land in Christine's
mouth.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:17.970
While the MPAA allows for depictions of violence
in PG-13 movies, the line is drawn at "both

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.270
realistic and extreme or persistent."

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:24.970
Since arms down throats and exploding eyes
aren't exactly realistic depictions of violence,

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:29.340
it makes sense that "Drag Me to Hell" was
able to drag itself out of the clutches of

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.759
an R rating.

00:08:30.759 --> 00:08:34.570
That doesn't make this scene — or the several
others depicting animal sacrifice — any

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.171
easier to sit through, though.

00:08:36.331 --> 00:08:37.331
"No.

00:08:37.331 --> 00:08:38.409
No, of course not."

